before:
Save score.h with the guarded declaration and score.c with its definition below. Each .c file plus included headers forms a translation unit. Compile gcc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror main.c score.c -o app, then run ./app. A declaration alone does not provide the linked function body.
```c
#ifndef SCORE_H
#define SCORE_H
int score(int value);
#endif

/* score.c */
#include "score.h"
int score(int value) { return value * 2; }
```

Complete local C example; the app itself does not execute it:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "score.h"
#include "score.h"

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", score(6));
    return 0;
}
```

after:
Save the three parts as score.h, score.c, and main.c. They make one program.
```c
// File: score.h
#ifndef SCORE_H
#define SCORE_H
int score(int value);
#endif

// File: score.c
#include "score.h"
int score(int value) { return value * 2; }

// File: main.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "score.h"
#include "score.h"

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", score(6));
    return 0;
}
```
